# Copperplate: the seven basic strokes, and letters built from them.
#
# Copperplate is written with a pointed pen whose tines spread under
# pressure, so a stroke swells and thins along its length. chi has no
# pressure — but a node's *width* is an ordinary expression, so pressure is
# just that expression, and the fitter draws the swell between the nodes.
# The pressure profile is sampled at nodes exactly as an outline is sampled
# at points.
#
# Note what is not used here: `pen`. A pen block is top level only and has
# no expression form, so one cannot be built inside a def — a nib that
# varies per node is not sayable. It is also not needed. The default nib
# axis is perpendicular to travel, which is what the tines do: the slit runs
# along the direction of travel and they open across it. `width` alone is
# the pointed pen.
#
# What a pen *would* have added is `tip` and `tail`, and those are round
# caps, which a pointed pen does not leave. Terminals here are the two the
# pen actually makes — a flat cut, which is the banks joined straight across
# the travel, and a sharp one, which is pressure 0, where the banks meet.
#
# Nothing is authored twice. Each stroke declares its pen path — the
# gesture, three or four points — and then places pressure *along* it:
#
#     e = press(spine, 2.65, 1);
#
# reads as "at 2.65 of the way along, full weight". Both the position and
# the direction come from the path, so the banks are true offsets of the
# curve that renders rather than of angles guessed alongside it. Sampling
# density is then free, which matters: an arch shoulder turns 125° in one
# segment, and without nodes inside the turn the inner bank has nothing to
# trace and folds over itself.
#
# A letter is one contour per pen stroke, all counterclockwise, overlapping
# where the strokes meet. The nonzero rule unions them — which is also how
# the letter is written, one stroke at a time.
#
# Where it runs out
#   * Pressure is continuous but its *shape* between two samples is not
#     chosen, it is whatever the fitter does to the banks. Where a stroke
#     reaches full weight and how quickly it lets go is most of what
#     distinguishes one hand from another, and the only control over it here
#     is placing more samples.
#   * Pressure and curvature interact, and nothing says so. Widening the pen
#     where the path turns tightest folds the inner bank over itself and
#     bites a wedge out of the stroke; an arch shoulder is exactly such a
#     place. The rule is that half the width must stay under the radius of
#     curvature, which copperplate obeys for free — you press on the
#     straight pull and release into the turns — but here it is a trap with
#     no diagnostic, and the symptom is a shape that looks gnawed. Every
#     light shoulder below is load-bearing.
#   * A stroke's two banks are written out in full, forwards and back,
#     because a def cannot take a variable number of nodes and a path cannot
#     be spliced into another path. Cheaper than it was once the round caps
#     went, but still the bulk of each stroke.
#   * The pen has no memory of its own angle. A real pointed pen spreads
#     along a fixed axis — the slit's — not perpendicular to travel, so its
#     hairlines are thinner across the slit than along it. Saying that needs
#     `nib` with a fixed angle, which then collapses the stroke to nothing
#     where travel runs parallel to it. What the shape wants is an ellipse
#     whose width varies per node, and that is exactly the pen that cannot
#     be built.
#   * A stroke is a macro, not geometry: 'm' re-runs an overturn twice
#     rather than placing one twice.
#   * Nothing carries the notion of pressure *rising*. The compound stroke
#     swells twice and both swells are placed by hand.
#   * Seven strokes are not an alphabet. The five letters below are the
#     combinations the reference spells out, and they cost nothing beyond
#     placing strokes — but the rest of the lowercase needs the three more
#     strokes it also names: the dot, the flat top, and the small underturn.
#     The combinatorial promise is real; the count is ten, not seven.

params {
  slant    = 55 in 40..70;    # the shade axis, degrees from the baseline
  shade    = 46 in 12..90;    # full-pressure thickness
  contrast = 11 in 3..24;     # shade / hairline
  xheight  = 250 in 150..320;
}

metrics {
  upm  = 1000;
  x    = xheight;
  asc  = 2.6 xheight;         # engrosser's proportions are extreme
  desc = -1.3 xheight;
  cap  = 2 xheight;
}

hair  = shade / contrast;
shear = 1 / tan(slant);       # rightward push per unit of height
aw    = 0.66 x;               # arch width: the run of one letter part
sb    = 0.18 x;               # sidebearing

touch = 0;         # the lightest mark: the tines only just apart

axis_up   = slant;            # travelling up the shade axis
axis_down = slant + 180;      # and down it

# A point `run` along the baseline and `rise` above it, pushed right by the
# slant, so that a fixed run traces the shade axis.
def at(run, rise) -> p { p = (run + shear * rise, rise); }

# A pressure sample: a node on the pen path at segment time `t`, its width
# the tine spread at `pressure` — 0 for a shut pen, 1 for full weight.
# Position and direction both come from the path.
def press(path, t, pressure) -> n {
  node n = point(path, t) dir direction(path, t)
           width hair + pressure * (shade - hair);
}

# ---- The seven strokes -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Terminals are what the pen leaves. A flat cut is the two banks joined by a
# straight line across the travel; a sharp one is pressure 0, where the banks
# meet and the stroke comes to a point.

# (1) The entrance stroke: hairline throughout, swinging up from the
# baseline to meet the letter. It begins from nothing and ends into the
# letter's first stroke.
def entrance(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = (run, 0.03 x) {30} .. (run + 0.45 aw, 0.24 x)
       .. at(run + 0.55 aw, 0.52 x) {axis_up};
  a = press(spine, 0,   0);
  b = press(spine, 1,   touch);
  c = press(spine, 1.6, touch);
  d = press(spine, 2,   touch);

  outline = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right -- d.left
         .. c.left .. b.left .. a.left -- cycle;
}

# (2) The oval: a closed pen path, shaded down its left flank and hairline
# up its right. No terminals at all. Travelling counterclockwise, the right
# bank is the outer contour and the left bank — reversed — is the counter.
def oval(run) -> (outer, counter, spine) {
  cu = run + 0.5 aw;
  ru = 0.38 aw;
  spine = at(cu, x) .. at(cu - ru, 0.5 x) .. at(cu, 0) .. at(cu + ru, 0.5 x)
       .. cycle;
  a = press(spine, 0,   0.5);     # top
  b = press(spine, 0.5, 1);
  c = press(spine, 1,   1);       # left flank: the shade
  d = press(spine, 1.5, 0.5);
  e = press(spine, 2,   touch);   # bottom
  f = press(spine, 2.5, touch);
  g = press(spine, 3,   touch);   # right flank
  h = press(spine, 3.5, touch);

  outer = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
       .. g.right .. h.right .. cycle;
  counter = a.left .. h.left .. g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left
       .. c.left .. b.left .. cycle;
}

# (3) The underturn: a flat top, down the shade axis under pressure, round
# the baseline as the pen lets go, away to a point.
def underturn(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = at(run, x) {axis_down} .. (run + 0.30 x, 0.14 x)
       .. {right} (run + 0.5 aw, 0) .. at(run + aw, 0.52 x) {axis_up};
  a = press(spine, 0,    0.55);   # the flat top
  b = press(spine, 0.35, 1);
  c = press(spine, 0.7,  1);
  d = press(spine, 1,    0.5);
  e = press(spine, 1.5,  touch);
  f = press(spine, 2,    touch);
  g = press(spine, 3,    0);

  outline = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
         .. g.right -- g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left .. c.left
         .. b.left .. a.left -- cycle;
}

# (4) The overturn: up from nothing, over the shoulder, then down under
# pressure to a flat foot. The mirror of the underturn, pressure and all.
def overturn(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = (run, 0.03 x) {50} .. at(run + 0.1 aw, 0.6 x)
       .. {right} at(run + aw, x) .. at(run + aw, 0.03 x) {axis_down};
  a = press(spine, 0,    0);
  b = press(spine, 1,    touch);
  c = press(spine, 2,    touch);  # the shoulder stays light
  d = press(spine, 2.4,  0.5);
  e = press(spine, 2.65, 1);
  f = press(spine, 2.85, 1);
  g = press(spine, 3,    0.55);

  outline = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
         .. g.right -- g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left .. c.left
         .. b.left .. a.left -- cycle;
}

# (5) The compound: an overturn and an underturn in one pen stroke — up,
# over, down under pressure, round the bottom, away. Two pressure events on
# one path, which is where the want of a dynamic nib shows most.
def compound(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = (run, 0.03 x) {50} .. at(run + 0.1 aw, 0.6 x)
       .. {right} at(run + aw, x) .. (run + aw + 0.30 x, 0.14 x)
       .. {right} (run + 1.5 aw, 0) .. at(run + 2 aw, 0.52 x) {axis_up};
  a = press(spine, 0,    0);
  b = press(spine, 1,    touch);
  c = press(spine, 2,    touch);  # the shoulder stays light
  d = press(spine, 2.45, 0.5);
  e = press(spine, 2.75, 1);
  f = press(spine, 3,    0.5);
  g = press(spine, 3.5,  touch);
  h = press(spine, 4,    touch);
  i = press(spine, 5,    0);

  outline = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
         .. g.right .. h.right .. i.right -- i.left .. h.left .. g.left
         .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left .. c.left .. b.left .. a.left
         -- cycle;
}

# (6) The ascender: a hairline loop over the top, then the shaded stem down
# to a flat foot on the baseline, crossing the loop on the way.
def ascender(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  lu = run + 0.34 aw;
  spine = at(lu, 0.5 asc) {72} .. at(lu + 0.3 aw, 0.84 asc)
       .. {170} at(lu + 0.02 aw, asc) .. at(lu - 0.26 aw, 0.8 asc)
       .. at(run, 0.42 asc) .. at(run, 0.03 x) {axis_down};
  a = press(spine, 0,   0);
  b = press(spine, 1,   touch);
  c = press(spine, 2,   touch);
  d = press(spine, 2.6, touch);
  e = press(spine, 3,   0.5);
  f = press(spine, 3.6, 1);
  g = press(spine, 4,   1);
  h = press(spine, 5,   0.5);

  outline = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
         .. g.right .. h.right -- h.left .. g.left .. f.left .. e.left
         .. d.left .. c.left .. b.left .. a.left -- cycle;
}

# (7) The descender: a flat top, the shaded stem down through the baseline,
# then the loop swinging out below and back up to a point, crossing the stem.
def descender(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = at(run, x) {axis_down} .. at(run, 0.42 desc)
       .. {185} at(run - 0.34 aw, desc) .. at(run - 0.74 aw, 0.42 desc)
       .. (run + 0.34 aw, 0.08 x) {28};
  a = press(spine, 0,    0.55);   # the flat top
  b = press(spine, 0.35, 1);
  c = press(spine, 0.75, 1);
  d = press(spine, 1,    0.5);
  e = press(spine, 1.5,  touch);
  f = press(spine, 2,    touch);
  g = press(spine, 3,    touch);
  h = press(spine, 4,    0);

  outline = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
         .. g.right .. h.right -- h.left .. g.left .. f.left .. e.left
         .. d.left .. c.left .. b.left .. a.left -- cycle;
}

# ---- The strokes as glyphs -------------------------------------------------
#
# Each shows its own pen path as a guide, so --debug reads as the gesture
# plus the pressure its nodes carry.

glyph entrance {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = entrance(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph oval {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (body, hole, k) = oval(sb);
  contour outer = body;
  contour inner = hole;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph underturn {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = underturn(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph overturn {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = overturn(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph compound {
  advance = 2 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = compound(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph ascender {
  advance = aw + shear * asc + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = ascender(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph descender {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 4 sb;
  (o, k) = descender(sb + 0.8 aw);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

# ---- Letters, as the strokes that write them ------------------------------

# (6) ascender + (5) compound
glyph h : U+0068 {
  advance = 2 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (stem, stem_path) = ascender(sb);
  (arch, arch_path) = compound(sb + 0.05 aw);
  contour s1 = stem;
  contour s2 = arch;
  guide k1 = stem_path;
  guide k2 = arch_path;
}

# (5) compound + (3) underturn. No stroke (1): the compound opens with an
# upstroke of its own, which is the entrance.
glyph u : U+0075 {
  advance = 3 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (first, first_path) = compound(sb);
  (last, last_path)   = underturn(sb + 2 aw);
  contour s1 = first;
  contour s2 = last;
  guide k1 = first_path;
  guide k2 = last_path;
}

# (4) overturn + (4) overturn + (5) compound
glyph m : U+006D {
  advance = 4 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (first, first_path)   = overturn(sb);
  (second, second_path) = overturn(sb + aw);
  (last, last_path)     = compound(sb + 2 aw);
  contour s1 = first;
  contour s2 = second;
  contour s3 = last;
  guide k1 = first_path;
  guide k2 = second_path;
  guide k3 = last_path;
}

# (4) overturn + (5) compound — the same two strokes 'm' ends with.
glyph n : U+006E {
  advance = 3 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (first, first_path) = overturn(sb);
  (last, last_path)   = compound(sb + aw);
  contour s1 = first;
  contour s2 = last;
  guide k1 = first_path;
  guide k2 = last_path;
}

# (1) entrance + (2) oval + (7) descender. The oval opens on a downstroke,
# so this is a letter that does need the entrance stroke.
glyph g : U+0067 {
  advance = 2 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (lead, lead_path)       = entrance(sb - 0.5 aw);
  (body, hole, body_path) = oval(sb);
  (tail, tail_path)       = descender(sb + 0.95 aw);
  contour s1 = lead;
  contour s2 = body;
  contour s3 = hole;
  contour s4 = tail;
  guide k1 = body_path;
  guide k2 = tail_path;
}
